        "costUsd": 0.0866572,
        "hasCode": true,
        "reasoning": "I'm designing a bioluminescent alien plant inspired by Pandora/Avatar aesthetics — a tall stalk with glowing fronds, spiral helicoradian-like shapes, bell flowers, glowing orbs, and roots, keeping it as a static model without needing joints since the hooks can accept a null bone reference.\n\nPlanning the construction: a tapering indigo trunk with glowing cyan torus bands, a crown of six curved fronds built from swept ellipse cross-sections along bezier paths, four small spiral plants around the base with glowing tips, a lathed magenta bell flower with a glowing stamen on top, and floating glowing spore orbs, with roots swept down so their lowest points rest right at y=0.\n\nStill working out the trunk geometry...\n\nStill shaping the fronds and crown...\n\nI'll design the bell-shaped crown flower as a lathe profile opening upward with stamens as glowing sweeps topped by small spheres, plus a central radiant orb above the trunk.\n\nFor ground detail, I'm adding small helical spiral sprouts scattered around the base, rendered as thin round-capped sweeps touching the ground, colored white-pink with a soft glow, alongside scattered tiny luminous bulbs of varying size for a mushroom-like scatter effect.\n\nNow I'm adding a handful of floating spore spheres at varying heights above the ground, plus scattering small glowing dots across the trunk's surface using a stick-and-scatter approach. I'm also naming the key parts—trunk, upper/lower fronds, bell—so I can apply texture requests to each, and starting to define colors for the trunk.\n\nWriting frond loop code...\n\nAdding more fronds with wider spacing and glow tip orbs, then tapering the trunk radius from base to tip. Setting the frond start point inside the trunk and finalizing the glow import path for the kit.\n\n"
      },
